1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,866
Twitch emotes are a
community's shared language.

2
00:00:03,367 --> 00:00:07,464
Emote Exchange turns that attention
into a game the whole chat can play:

3
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:12,027
a live emote market, using free
play Coins with no cash value.

4
00:00:12,376 --> 00:00:17,019
The streamer runs one local app. From
this dashboard, they connect Twitch,

5
00:00:17,055 --> 00:00:20,351
manage the market, and add
a browser source to OBS.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:25,645
The overlay shows Prices, top Traders,
recent activity and market moments.

7
00:00:25,936 --> 00:00:28,292
Here is the real Twitch
connection in action.

8
00:00:28,583 --> 00:00:31,868
A viewer buys two shares by
typing a command in chat.

9
00:00:32,218 --> 00:00:36,674
The app processes the Trade, saves
the Holding, updates the Feed, and

10
00:00:36,699 --> 00:00:40,610
replies through the bot. The portfolio
command reports the viewer's

11
00:00:40,635 --> 00:00:44,825
Coins and shares. Selling settles
the Trade and updates the balance

12
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:49,980
again. These are actual chat messages
and app responses, recorded together.

13
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:53,869
Two different chatters using a
recognized emote can list it.

14
00:00:54,219 --> 00:00:58,153
Prices follow its share of recent chat
attention, with diminishing credit

15
00:00:58,178 --> 00:01:02,913
for repeated Uses from one person. The
streamer can pause trading, moderate

16
00:01:02,938 --> 00:01:07,209
Traders and give Grants. Greetings
reward returning participation with

17
00:01:07,234 --> 00:01:11,632
Streaks. Live analytics separates
real chat from simulation.

18
00:01:11,982 --> 00:01:16,486
For larger crowds, this clearly labeled
SIM run exercises the same engine.

19
00:01:16,986 --> 00:01:21,210
The keyless demo works without Twitch
credentials, and simulated activity

20
00:01:21,235 --> 00:01:25,158
never sends messages to Twitch.
Codex helped build the project from

21
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:27,573
its specifications and acceptance checks.

22
00:01:27,923 --> 00:01:32,530
The market engine is pure: time comes
in, and persistence, display and

23
00:01:32,555 --> 00:01:37,278
reply effects come out. A real review
found that simulated buys happened

24
00:01:37,303 --> 00:01:41,760
before emotes were listed. The scenario
was corrected to establish Listings

25
00:01:41,785 --> 00:01:47,090
first. Automated tests cover settlement,
recovery and output isolation;

26
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:52,569
Windows and Ubuntu CI both pass.
Emote Exchange makes chat attention

27
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:56,877
visible, gives viewers a shared game,
and keeps the streamer in control.

28
00:01:57,296 --> 00:02:01,567
One local process, a Twitch
chat, and an OBS browser source.
